WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.680
Few actors have been lucky enough to feature in a&nbsp;
universally-acclaimed movie, and fewer still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.680
appeared in many — but it does happen. From major&nbsp;
movie stars to little-known character performers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.720
these are the actors who have the most movies&nbsp;
with a 100 percent score on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.520
In 1959, Alec Guinness was awarded a knighthood&nbsp;
by Elizabeth II for his contribution to the field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.880
of arts — just in case his Academy Award, his&nbsp;
BAFTA, his star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.560
and his nine perfect movies on Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;
weren't testament enough to his talent.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.600
Like many other esteemed actors, Alec Guinness&nbsp;
enjoyed working with one particular director.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.080
The British thespian was a preferred collaborator&nbsp;
for the acclaimed director David Lean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.640
often appearing in his legendary screen&nbsp;
adaptations of Charles Dickens films, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.680
Great Expectations and Oliver Twist. Both of these&nbsp;
films are among Guiness' 100% movies, which also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.560
includes two of the darkest comedies ever made&nbsp;
— The Ladykillers and Kind Hearts and Coronets.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.480
Of course, Guinness will forever&nbsp;
be remembered as Obi-Wan Kenobi in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.120
George Lucas' original Star Wars trilogy&nbsp;
— but that movie isn't one of the nine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.840
boasting an impressive yet&nbsp;
imperfect 92% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.800
"Toto, I've a feeling we're&nbsp;
not in Kansas anymore."

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.040
Judy Garland may well be one of the most famous&nbsp;
actresses of all time. Though she's perhaps best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.720
known as Dorothy in 1939's The Wizard of&nbsp;
Oz, which sits at 98% on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.240
Garland has appeared in a total of nine films with&nbsp;
a perfect score — including the Christmas classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.480
Meet Me in St. Louis and other beloved musicals&nbsp;
such as Summer Stock and Till the Clouds Roll By.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.080
Garland is yet another critically renowned star&nbsp;
who took on a new name during her youth, in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.880
case from Frances Ethel Gumm. While performing&nbsp;
with her siblings as the singing Gumm Sisters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.400
a fellow performer suggested they change the&nbsp;
name. The Gumm Sisters became the Garland Sisters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.520
and Judy chose her new first name based on&nbsp;
inspiration from a Hoagy Carmichael song.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.600
Unfortunately, this was just the first&nbsp;
of many demands showbiz would come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.000
place on Garland. In the end, Garland was&nbsp;
overcome by the pressures of the industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.320
the abusive nature of the people she worked with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.840
and the substance abuse that followed. She&nbsp;
died of an accidental overdose in 1969.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.800
Having been forced to fend for herself at a&nbsp;
very young age, it makes sense that Barbara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.120
Stanwyck would be best known for playing&nbsp;
complex and independent women on screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.880
She began working at just 13 years old, first as a&nbsp;
chorus girl, then a nightclub dancer, and finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:43.200
with touring companies. She then landed her first&nbsp;
Broadway play in 1926 with The Noose, in which she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.640
played the role of a cabaret dancer. Her leading&nbsp;
role in the 1927 run of Burlesque earned Stanwyck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.760
her first movie offers, marking the beginning of&nbsp;
a long career in Hollywood; one that included such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.240
films as The Lady Eve, which is just one of her&nbsp;
nine films with a 100% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.760
Stanwyck was one of the most versatile,&nbsp;
adaptable actresses in the history of cinema.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.360
She even changed her name at the alleged&nbsp;
behest of a producer on The Noose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.800
having been born Ruby Catherine Stevens. Because&nbsp;
she was known for that versatility — rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.600
the movie star glamour that was the typical&nbsp;
of female leads at the time — she was often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.800
passed over for actresses like Bette Davis&nbsp;
or Jean Arthur. But none of those stars ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.080
appeared in more truly great movies than this&nbsp;
self-sufficient queen of stage and screen.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.480
Born Samuel Jones in 1898, the man later&nbsp;
known as Wallace Ford ran away from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.800
abusive home and joined a vaudeville troupe&nbsp;
traveling through Canada. As a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.280
he journeyed south by train-hopping to the U.S.&nbsp;
in search of a better life with his friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.360
who was actually named Wallace Ford.&nbsp;
Tragically, Ford was run over by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.520
locomotive, and Jones then took the&nbsp;
name of his dead friend as tribute.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.960
And what a tribute it was. From theater troupes&nbsp;
to Broadway to Hollywood, Ford made a home in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.760
the entertainment industry just as he made a&nbsp;
home in America. He became known as a tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:10.400
but friendly and quick-witted comic character,&nbsp;
who starred in over 150 TV series and films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.080
nine of which were 100% loved by critics.&nbsp;
These included Alfred Hitchcock's Shadow of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.200
a Doubt and the surprisingly dark Jimmy&nbsp;
Stewart Western, The Man from Laramie.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.800
Gary Cooper's characters were often soft-spoken&nbsp;
and stoic, yet always heroic. This unique persona&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.080
led the man born as Frank James Cooper to become&nbsp;
a much-loved star in the entertainment industry.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.760
With his iconic drawl and imposing height, Cooper&nbsp;
was the perfect pick for the Western genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.280
so it should come as no surprise that titles like&nbsp;
The Westerner, The Virginian, and The Plainsman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.360
are all riding tall in the 100% Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;
saddle. He also starred in war films such as Beau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.240
Geste and comedies like Ball of Fire, but if&nbsp;
you really want to understand Cooper's charm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.480
you have to look outside his 100% line-up&nbsp;
— and focus on 1941's Meet John Doe.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.280
In a roundabout way, this movie says the most&nbsp;
about the types of characters Cooper portrayed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.680
Rather than the elite Hollywood actor&nbsp;
whose star power leaked into every role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.840
audiences got to know Cooper as&nbsp;
the everyman; the literal John Doe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.920
who reflected the dreams and aspirations&nbsp;
of the everyday average American.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.160
It makes sense that Humphrey Bogart be&nbsp;
closely associated with the notion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:30.000
Hollywood perfection, considering he's the iconic&nbsp;
face of the 1942 film so frequently cited as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.280
epitome of "good cinema": Casablanca.&nbsp;
In fact, renowned critic Roger Ebert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:41.120
once suggested Casablanca appears on more "best&nbsp;
films of all time" lists than any other movie.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.600
Surprisingly enough, however, Casablanca scores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.360
99% on Rotten Tomatoes, meaning it doesn't&nbsp;
quite make it into Bogart's 100 Club.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.000
Still, he has an impressive 11&nbsp;
perfect-scoring movies to make up for it.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.480
One of those films, 1941's The Maltese Falcon,&nbsp;
catapulted Bogart to a whole new level of success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.120
Having slowly worked his way through B-movies&nbsp;
and played supporting roles in bigger flicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:11.120
Bogart was suddenly a leading man — and he'd&nbsp;
soon become known as one of the all-time greats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.480
It's interesting to note, however, that he&nbsp;
had an exactly equal number of 100%-rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.520
films before and after The Maltese Falcon,&nbsp;
a testament to the fact that he'd always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.320
been an asset to the productions in which he&nbsp;
featured, whether he was a leading man or not.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.000
"Here's looking at you, kid."

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.920
Funnily enough, the legendary actor C. Aubrey&nbsp;
Smith started out as a cricketer — and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.480
sportsmanlike conduct of the game easily&nbsp;
translated to the gentlemanly characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:44.560
he'd become known for in Hollywood. Smith appeared&nbsp;
in 11 perfect-scoring movies during his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.520
such as The Four Feathers and And Then There&nbsp;
Were None, and in the majority of them he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.720
portrayed either an aristocrat or a military&nbsp;
officer. Indeed, it seems like Smith never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:59.840
quite forgot his roots. Even after arriving&nbsp;
in California, he insisted that his English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.040
colleagues all join the Hollywood Cricket Club&nbsp;
— and make a point to show up and play, too.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.680
In addition to being renowned&nbsp;
for the roles he played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.960
Smith was also famous for his facial hair,&nbsp;
which included a set of bushy eyebrows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.320
an impressive handlebar mustache. Along with&nbsp;
his cricket obsession, that impressive 'stache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.800
fed back into the persona of the Hollywood&nbsp;
gentleman. And sure, maybe that meant Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.760
was a little typecast — but it clearly&nbsp;
served him well during his 34-year career.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.040
From his early beginnings at the Omaha&nbsp;
Community Playhouse, Henry Fonda rose to great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.280
prominence in the entertainment industry — and&nbsp;
eventually played in 11 films that received 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.680
approval ratings from critics on Rotten Tomatoes.&nbsp;
He appeared in a number of productions now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:50.320
regarded as classics, such as The Lady Eve and&nbsp;
the iconic courtroom thriller 12 Angry Men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.120
His working relationship with John Ford&nbsp;
also produced a good few films with 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.920
ratings, including The Grapes of Wrath,&nbsp;
Young Mr. Lincoln, and My Darling Clementine.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:05.120
Fonda's range was exceptionally wide, not&nbsp;
just in terms of acting ability but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.320
the projects he featured in, too. In fact,&nbsp;
he's the only actor with a number of 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.600
films in the double digits who&nbsp;
also appeared in a film with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.840
0% approval rating on Rotten Tomatoes:&nbsp;
the much-maligned 1977 movie Tentacles.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.960
But Fonda's legacy is carried by his family as&nbsp;
well as his films. He's the father of decorated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.480
actress and activist Jane Fonda, as well as actor&nbsp;
and director Peter Fonda. His Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.440
profile describes him as having a "benign,&nbsp;
paternal presence," and despite being somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.960
distant as an actual father, he clearly instilled&nbsp;
a genuine love of acting into his children.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.880
Audiences saw the actor and military officer&nbsp;
James Stewart as the embodiment of the ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:51.920
American — something of a nebulous accolade&nbsp;
today, but back then it made him one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.240
most popular movie stars around. He was&nbsp;
clearly a hit with the critics, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.200
having appeared in a dozen pictures that&nbsp;
are currently 100% favorably reviewed.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.840
The moral rectitude of his early roles may have&nbsp;
been part of what made the moral obscurity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:11.680
his later roles all the more striking. Though they&nbsp;
aren't counted among his perfect-scoring pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.120
Stewart starred in a number of legendary Hitchcock&nbsp;
films, often as a far shadier character than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:22.640
those he was better-known for playing. That's&nbsp;
also true for The Naked Spur, a 100%-scoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.800
Western that found Stewart playing a&nbsp;
bounty hunter with a serious dark side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.320
This trend continued with movies such as&nbsp;
The Man from Laramie and Winchester '73,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:38.480
which cemented Stewart's reputation as one of&nbsp;
the most versatile and popular actors of his era.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.280
One of the most recognizable names&nbsp;
in cinema history also boasts a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.560
whopping 12 movies with a 100% Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes score. Marion Robert Morrison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.720
known professionally by his screen&nbsp;
name John Wayne, is almost synonymous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:58.320
with the American Western, and he was a&nbsp;
sure-fire box-office hit for many decades.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.720
None of this might ever have happened, however,&nbsp;
if Marion Robert Morrison the football player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.680
hadn't suffered a career-ending injury. With&nbsp;
his sporting dreams dashed, Morrison turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:13.200
to Hollywood for his shot at greatness. His very&nbsp;
first leading role was, of course, in a Western:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:19.680
1930's The Big Trail. But the picture which made&nbsp;
him famous didn't arrive until nine years later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:26.000
John Ford's 1939 movie Stagecoach featured Wayne&nbsp;
in the role of the Ringo Kid, and subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.640
made him a household name. Fittingly, both&nbsp;
of these films have earned 100% approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:36.640
ratings on Rotten Tomatoes, along with other such&nbsp;
classics as Rio Bravo, Red River, and El Dorado.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.360
Ward Bond has the most 100%-scoring&nbsp;
movies of any actor in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:47.600
which appears to be one of the great&nbsp;
benefits of being a character actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.880
Bond didn't often play the lead in his films,&nbsp;
but his status as an indispensable supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.840
character led to the record breadth of&nbsp;
his career in universally-beloved films.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:04.720
As a result, Bond has played in a stunning&nbsp;
15 films with 100% on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:10.080
including The Grapes of Wrath, The Maltese&nbsp;
Falcon, and Young Mr. Lincoln. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.400
he starred in almost two dozen movies&nbsp;
alongside his former football teammate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.840
John Wayne, and often worked with preferred&nbsp;
collaborators such as John Ford and Frank Capra.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.680
On top of that, he has appeared in&nbsp;
a number of all-time classics with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.240
slightly less than perfect scores, such&nbsp;
as It's a Wonderful Life and Gone with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.520
Wind. So while Ward Bond may not have been the&nbsp;
most famous actor to have worked in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:38.720
it's unlikely anyone will ever quite&nbsp;
manage to match his incredible achievement.

